  "schema_version": "2.0.0",
  "class_level": 11,
  "concept_tier": "simple",
  "source_book": "NCERT Class 11 Ch.5 Laws of Motion — table-of-contents reference only (Newton's third law is its own section, immediately after the second law, before constraint/pulley problems); teaching authored from first principles. REBUILD (2026-07-29, feat/lom-b) of a founder-rejected v1 (two 300 kg blocks, an asserted 30 N pair with no on-screen interaction object). The atomic idea: every force is one half of a single two-body interaction — the two halves are always equal in magnitude, opposite in direction, act at the same instant, and act on DIFFERENT bodies, which is why they can never cancel. Does NOT cover how much a force accelerates a body (newton_second_law, used here only as the a = F/m read-off), complete force diagrams (free_body_diagram), friction or inclines (block_on_incline), string-coupled bodies (connected_bodies), or momentum conservation. Engine: the shared newtons_laws_body field_3d scenario, extended 2026-07-29 (commit 248abea) with push_off (contact-then-release force phase), spring (the visible interaction object), and fixed (wall/Earth-anchored body) — docs/NLB_PUSH_OFF_SPEC.md + docs/loop_runs/push_off_report.md. The new 5-state arc makes the interaction itself an on-screen object (a compressed spring) rather than asserting a force pair that appears from nowhere. All five states are expressible with the engine's existing config surface; ENGINE GAP: none.",
